<fig:02>

The observed behavior of $h$ can be understood based on kinetic gas theory considering thermal transport in a gas from the continuum regime at high pressures ($K n < 0.01$) to the free-molecular regime at low pressures ($K n > 10$) as determined by the value of the Knudsen number $K n = l_(upright("mfp")) \/ d$ \[#link(<ref-Springer1971>)[32]\]. For an ideal gas, the mean free path of gas molecules may be written as $l_(upright("mfp")) = k_(upright("B")) T \/ sqrt(2) pi d_(upright("g"))^2 p = zeta \/ p$ according to kinetic gas theory. Here, $k_(upright("B"))$ is the Boltzmann constant, and $d_(upright("g"))$ is the kinetic diameter of a gas molecule. In Appendix~D we derive an expression for $h$ based on an infinitely long-wire model that subdivides the space around the wire into two concentric regions: one close to the wire with thickness $l_(upright(m f p))$ in which molecules may collide with the wire but not with each other, and a second farther away from the wire in which they do collide with each other and continuum conduction holds (see inset of @fig:03\(b)). An expression for $h$ derived from a similar model for spherical particles has been suggested to hold for all $K n$ \[#link(<ref-Yuen1986>)[33]\]. Unlike in the spherical case, the logarithmic form of the temperature profile in our cylindrical geometry requires the introduction of a new length $r_0 gt.double d$ at which the temperature of the gas reaches $T \( r_0 \) = T_0$. We obtain $ h = frac(kappa_(upright(g a s)), frac(d l_(upright(m f p)) pi^2, 4 alpha_(upright(w)) Phi \( d + 2 l_(upright(m f p)) \) arcsin (frac(d, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) + d / 2 ln (frac(2 r_0, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) \, $<eq:hmodel-new> where $alpha_(upright(w))$ is the accommodation coefficient of the wire, describing the fractional extent to which molecules colliding with the wire have their mean energy adjusted to its temperature ($0 lt.eq alpha_(upright(w)) lt.eq 1$), and $Phi$ is a numerical coefficient that depends on the atomicity of the gas molecules. Taking nitrogen (the main component of air) as the gas in our experiment, we use its room-temperature thermal conductivity $kappa_(upright(g a s)) = 0.026$~W/(m$dot.op$K) \[#link(<ref-huber2011thermal>)[34]\], and kinetic diameter $d_(upright("g")) = 364$~pm going forward. $Phi = 48 \/ 95$ for such a diatomic gas \[#link(<ref-Yuen1986>)[33]\]. Note that in the continuum regime ($K n arrow.r 0$) Equation~(@eq:hmodel-new) simplifies to $ h = frac(2 kappa_(upright(g a s)), d ln \( 2 r_0 \/ d \)) \, $<eq:hmodel-cont> i.e., the high-pressure value of $h$ does not depend on $alpha_(upright(w))$, but is set by $kappa_(upright(g a s))$ and $r_0$ instead.

