<fig:03>

@fig:03\(b) shows a fit of our data using Eq.~(@eq:hmodel-new) which yields $alpha_(upright(w)) = 1.09 plus.minus 0.04$ and $r_0 = \( 730 plus.minus 23 \)$~µm. Since $alpha_(upright(w))$ cannot actually exceed 1, we take our fit result as an indication that $alpha_(upright(w)) approx 1$ in our experiment, which compares with a value of 0.89 reported for nitrogen on Pt \[#link(<ref-Dayton1998>)[35]\]. Considering air as a gas mixture, we could alternatively treat $alpha_(upright(w)) Phi$ as a single fit parameter (still using $kappa_(upright(g a s)) = 0.026$~W/(m$dot.op$K) and $d_(upright(g)) = 364$~pm, though), yielding $alpha_(upright(w)) Phi = 0.55 plus.minus 0.02$ instead. With $alpha_(upright(w)) lt.eq 1$, this result would imply $Phi gt.eq 0.55$, which could indicate the presence of molecules with atomicity $> 2$ such as water. A more refined model and better defined surface condition of the studied wire would allow for more definitive conclusions. The value of $r_0$ on the other hand compares well with the distance between the suspended Pt wire section and the copper block in our setup, which is approximately equal to $500$~µm \[set by the thickness of our AlN substrate, see @fig:1\(a)\]. Although our infinite wire model does not imply a specific value of $r_0$, we note that it directly compares with a textbook model for a wire in a gas-filled concentric cylinder of radius $r_0$ \[#link(<ref-Dayton1998>)[35]\], $ h = frac(kappa_(upright(g a s)), beta' (frac(d, 2 r_0) + 1) l_(upright(m f p)) + d / 2 ln (frac(2 r_0, d))) \, $<eq:h> in that this model also simplifies to Eq.~(@eq:hmodel-cont) in the continuum limit. This suggests that $r_0$ should indeed be comparable with the distance between the wire axis and the nearest physical surface in our experiment. In Eq.~(@eq:h), $beta' = frac(2 - alpha', alpha') dot.op frac(9 gamma' - 5, 2 gamma' + 2)$ is a numerical value of order one, where $alpha'$ is the accommodation coefficient assumed identical for both wire and inside surface of the concentric cylinder, and $gamma' = c_p \/ c_v = 1.405$ for nitrogen at room temperature \[#link(<ref-Dayton1998>)[35]\]. Although our wire is not actually suspended inside a larger concentric cylinder, we obtain a good fit of our data using Equation~(@eq:h) with $beta'$ and $r_0$ as fit parameters, see the dotted line in @fig:03\(b). The fit yields $r_0 = \( 690 plus.minus 30 \)$~µm, which compares very well with the result obtained using Eq.~(@eq:hmodel-new) as expected. We further find $beta' = \( 3.7 plus.minus 0.2 \)$, which implies $alpha' = 0.60 plus.minus 0.03$. This result should be interpreted with caution, as our experiment does not provide the concentric cylinder surface with accommodation coefficient $alpha'$ required by Eq.~(@eq:h).

@fig:03\(c) plots $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$ as a function of the heat-transfer coefficient $h$. We find that the data agree well with a linear model (dashed line), as predicted by Eq.~(@eq:omega_inflection). From the fitted line, we extract $rho c_p = \( 2.92 plus.minus 0.11 \) times 10^6$~J/(m$""^3$K), in good agreement with the room-temperature value for Pt, $2.85 times 10^6$~J/(m$""^3$K) \[#link(<ref-Furukawa1974>)[31]\]. The value of $kappa = \( 81.2 plus.minus 6.0 \)$~W/(m$dot.op$K) determined from the presented fit based on Eq.~(@eq:omega_inflection) is consistent with $kappa = \( 75.8 plus.minus 5.7 \)$~W/(m$dot.op$K) that we used to calculate $h$ from our data in the first place. This $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$ analysis provides a method for extracting $rho c_p$ and $kappa$ from 3$omega$ measurements at finite gas pressure, provided a validated $h \( p \)$ model as derived above is employed.

#figure(image("source/PRApplied-3w-Fig4.png", width: 100%), placement: top,
  caption: [
    Volumetric heat capacity of the Pt wire determined under different gas pressures. Gray dashed line is $rho c_p = 2.85 times 10^6 med$J/(m$""^3$K) expected for Pt at 297~K \[#link(<ref-Furukawa1974>)[31]\].
  ]
)
<fig:04>

Next, we show that even without invoking a particular $h \( p \)$ model, it is still possible to determine $rho c_p$ from the measured data. Using Equation~(@eq:v3wrmsfinal), we first determine the slope of $tan phi.alt'$ versus $omega$ from a linear fit of the measured phases in @fig:02\(b) and subsequently fit the $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ data in @fig:02\(a) to determine $rho c_p$. It proves difficult to reliably measure the phase $phi.alt'$ of the diminishing $V_(3 omega)$ signal as the frequency increases. We therefore limit the $phi.alt'$ analysis to frequencies below 8~Hz. This frequency range extends well beyond the low-frequency plateau of $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ and includes the inflection frequency $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$ in all cases. Best fits of the $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ data are shown as dashed lines in @fig:02\(a), and extracted $rho c_p$ values are shown in @fig:04. These $rho c_p$ values compare very well with the value expected for Pt at 297~K. Overall, the $rho c_p$ values are consistent across 8 orders of magnitude in gas pressure with limited spread, about $plus.minus 10 %$. This demonstrates the feasibility of determining $rho c_p$ of a suspended wire even at finite $h$.


= Discussion
<discussion>
The heat-transfer coefficients $h$ extracted from our experiments inherently include interfacial thermal resistance effects. However, our analytical $h$ model does not resolve the underlying mechanisms governing thermal transport across the solid-gas interface. Instead, these complex interfacial phenomena are lumped into the accommodation coefficient $alpha$, which serves as a phenomenological parameter that captures the aggregate effect of interface-specific thermal transport processes. Direct simulation Monte Carlo (DSMC) \[#link(<ref-Bird1994>)[36], #link(<ref-Tantos2015>)[37]\] or Lorentz gas approaches \[#link(<ref-chen2018rough>)[38], #link(<ref-wang2019thermal>)[39]\] could provide deeper mechanistic insights but go beyond the scope of this work. Both should be amenable to similarly capturing the heat transfer across continuum to free-molecular gas regimes, albeit at non-negligible computational cost. DSMC could provide molecular-level resolution of surface-gas interactions, and could serve to reveal how wire surface properties, roughness, and nonequilibrium distributions may affect thermal resistance. The Lorentz gas model could offer a complementary approach to systematically investigate how geometric factors like wire diameter and surface morphology may influence interfacial scattering patterns \[#link(<ref-chen2018rough>)[38], #link(<ref-PhysRevE.103.052135>)[40]\].

Conducting 3$omega$ measurements at finite $h$ offers the advantage that larger $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ signal strengths may be achieved while maintaining a fixed maximum dc temperature rise, as illustrated in @fig:05\(a). This results essentially from the fact that $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s)) prop I_(upright(r m s))^3$ while $Delta T_(upright(d c)) prop I_(upright(r m s))^2$ (also $Delta T_(upright(a c)) prop I_(upright(r m s))^2$), as described in Equations~(#ref(label("eq:temp rise with gas"))) and (@eq:rms). The black dashed line in @fig:05\(a) shows a trajectory of constant maximum dc temperature rise (in the middle of the wire) of 2~K, along which $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ increases from 24~µV at $h = 0$ to 58~µV at $h = 700$~W/(m$""^2$K).

Such an enhancement may facilitate the characterization, e.g., of low thermal conductivity wires. Consider a fictitious wire that has the same properties as our Pt wire except for $kappa = 1$~W/(m$dot.op$K). To limit the maximum dc temperature rise to 2~K while $h = 0$, we would need to reduce the applied current to $I_(upright(r m s)) = 0.64$~mA. The resulting $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ would be only $2.9$~µV. If alternatively we wanted to measure at $I_(upright(r m s)) = 7.58$~mA used for most measurements of our Pt wire, we would have to shorten the wire length to $305$~µm. For this length estimation, we have used $R = 0.178 med Omega$ and $R' = 0.6 med upright(m Omega)$/K based on the 297-K values of $R$ and $R'$ determined for our 3.63-mm-long Pt wire and assumed both constant electrical resistivity and constant temperature coefficient of the electrical resistivity. At this shorter length, $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ would equally be $2.9$~µV. If we instead allowed for a finite $h$, e.g., $h = 700$~W/(m$""^2$K), we could maintain a maximum dc temperature rise of 2~K with $I_(upright(r m s)) = 11$~mA applied to the 3.63-mm-long wire. The resulting $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ would be $63$~µV, a more than an order of magnitude improvement in signal strength compared to the $h = 0$ case. We recall that increasing $h$ also increases $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$ as per Equation~(@eq:omega_inflection), and illustrate this in @fig:05\(b).

#figure(image("source/PRApplied-3w-Fig5.png", width: 100.0%),
  caption: [
    Benefit of finite $h$ for the thermal characterization of suspended wires. (a) Calculated $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ and (b) calculated $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$ for our Pt wire. Black dashed lines in (a) and (b) are trajectories for a maximum dc temperature rise of 2~K. (c) Calculated frequency dependence of $V_(upright(3 omega \, r m s))$ of a carbon nanotube with 20-nm outer diameter.
  ]
)
<fig:05>

In addition, introducing a finite $h$ can help measure $rho c_p$ for fine wires with diameters on the nanometer scale. While shortening the length of a suspended wire may not necessarily impede the characterization of $rho c_p$ of the wire when its diameter $d$ is on the micrometer scale, more careful consideration is required when $d$ is on the nanometer scale. To illustrate this, we consider the case of a 1.4-µm-long suspended carbon nanotube with outer and inner diameters of $d = 20$~nm and $d_(upright(i)) = 10$~nm, respectively, as studied by \[#link(<ref-Choi2006>)[25]\]. The authors applied currents on the order of 10~μA, and found $R = 9.36 med upright(k) Omega$ and $R' = - 14.8 med Omega$/K at $T = 297.5$~K. From 3$omega$ measurements, they determined $kappa = \( 300 plus.minus 20 \)$~W/(m$dot.op$K). However, they were not able to determine the volumetric heat capacity, which we estimate as $rho c_p = 8.5 times 10^4$~J/(m$""^3$K). For this estimate we use $c_p = 500$~J/(kg$dot.op$K) as the room-temperature carbon-nanotube-specific heat \[#link(<ref-Yi1999>)[24]\], and $rho = 1700$~kg/m$""^3$ as the effective-mass density considering a graphite mass density of $2260$~kg/m$""^3$ smeared over the entire volume of an effective solid wire with outer diameter $d = 20$~nm.

We plot the frequency dependence of $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ for the reported parameters and our $rho c_p$ estimate as well as $I_(upright(r m s)) = 10.55$~µA in @fig:05\(c) (black line). The curve remains flat up to approximately 100~MHz, way beyond the upper frequency limit of $50$~kHz in the study conducted by \[#link(<ref-Choi2006>)[25]\]. As seen from the low-frequency approximation (Equation~(@eq:v3wLF)), this regime does not provide sufficient information to extract $rho c_p$. Information on volumetric heat capacity can only be accessed when the measurement frequency is increased above 100~MHz, which falls in a more stringent radio-frequency (rf) regime where challenges including impedance matching requirements, parasitic capacitance and inductance effects may need to be properly addressed. Moreover, at such high frequencies, the thermal diffusion length becomes smaller than the tube diameter, causing temperature gradients across the tube cross section that violate the assumption of radially uniform heating underpinning Eq.~@eq:1D.

To achieve a $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s))$ curve which stops being constant at approximately 10~kHz rather than 100~MHz, the length $L$ of the nanotube must be increased. Calculated curves for $L = 500$~µm are shown in @fig:05\(c). As can be seen, to keep the dc temperature rise limited to 2~K at $h = 0$, a much smaller current of $I_(upright(r m s)) = 30$~nA is required (blue line). Reliably sourcing and measuring such a small current may be challenging and would likely require careful instrumentation and experiment design considerations. Allowing for $h = 300$~W/(m$""^2$K) instead would allow applying $I_(upright(r m s)) = 77$~nA (red line in @fig:05\(c)), which is still a small current but may be more easily sourced and measured compared to 30~nA. This would further increase $V_(3 omega \, upright(r m s \, L F))$ by approximately a factor of 3 as can be seen in @fig:05\(c). The associated increase in $omega_(upright(i n f l e c t i o n))$, however, may call for a balanced approach in optimizing signal strength and inflection frequency in 3$omega$ measurements of such suspended wires with nanoscale diameters. We note that for carbon nanotubes in air and at room temperature, an upper bound of $h$ on the order of 0.1~MW/(m$""^2 dot.op$K) has been suggested \[#link(<ref-Hu2007>)[41], #link(<ref-Hsu2011>)[42]\].

= Conclusions
<conclusions>
We conducted 3$omega$ measurements of a 16-µm-diameter Pt wire across pressures from 10$""^(- 5)$ to $10^3$~mbar, validating a 1D heat-transfer model that accounts for both the wire's thermal properties ($kappa$ and $rho c_p$) and gas-mediated heat loss through coefficient $h$. Our analytical solution extends established vacuum-based theory to finite gas environments, with $h$ accurately described by kinetic gas theory models across continuum to free-molecular regimes. A key finding is that volumetric heat capacity $rho c_p$ can be extracted to within $plus.minus 10 med %$ without requiring specific knowledge of $h \( p \)$, while use of validated $h \( p \)$ models would enable simultaneous determination of both $kappa$ and $rho c_p$. The finite gas-pressure approach offers practical advantages, including enhanced signal strength and reduced frequency requirements that make thermal characterization more accessible for low thermal conductivity materials and nanoscale wires where traditional vacuum methods may be limiting. This work establishes controlled gas environments as a valuable tool for 3$omega$ thermal measurements, expanding the applicability of the technique to challenging material systems.

