= Analytical Solution of the Heat-Transfer Equation
<analytical-solution-of-the-heat-transfer-equation>
We solve Equation~(@eq:1D) as follows. Assuming the current to have been turned on at a time $t = - oo$, the temperature variation from $T_0$ at any time can be calculated as the integral of the response of the temperature of the wire to the applied current at each instant of time as described by the equation $ Delta T \( x \, t \) = T \( x \, t \) - T_0 = integral_(- oo)^t z \( x \, t \; tau \) thin upright(d) tau . $<eq:impanalog> Here, $z \( x \, t \; tau \)$ satisfies $ frac(partial z, partial t) - frac(kappa, rho c_p) frac(partial^2 z, partial x^2) + frac(h C, S rho c_p) z - frac(I_0^2 R', L S rho c_p) z sin^2 \( omega t \) = 0 $<eq:difheat> and is subject to the boundary conditions $ {z \( 0 \, t \) = 0\
z \( L \, t \) = 0\
z \( x \, tau + 0 \) = frac(I_0^2 R, L S rho c_p) sin^2 \( omega tau \) $<difcon> Expanding $z$ as a Fourier series gives $ z \( x \, t \; tau \) = sum_(n = 1)^oo U_n \( t \; tau \) sin frac(n pi x, L) \, $<eq:fourier> which can then be substituted back into Equation~(@eq:difheat) to give $ sum_(n = 1)^oo [frac(upright(d) U_n, upright(d) t) + (n^2 / gamma + frac(h C, S rho c_p) - frac(I_0^2 R', L S rho c_p) sin^2 \( omega t \)) U_n] sin frac(n pi x, L) = 0\
 $<eq:difheatexp> with $gamma = L^2 \/ \( pi^2 alpha \)$ and $alpha = kappa \/ \( rho c_p \)$ as was done previously. Neglecting the $sin^2 \( omega t \)$ term, since in most cases $frac(I_0^2 R' L, n^2 pi^2 kappa S) lt.double 1$ \[#link(<ref-Lu2001>)[13]\], Equation~(@eq:difheatexp) can be solved to give $ U_n \( t \; tau \) = C_n \( tau \) e^(- (n^2 / gamma + frac(h C, S rho c_p)) (t - tau)) . $<eq:fouriercoeff> Using the relation $sum_(n = 1)^oo frac(2 [1 - \( - 1 \)^n], n pi) sin frac(n pi x, L) = 1$ for $0 < x < L$ and the boundary conditions in~(@difcon), $C_n$ can be determined as $ C_n \( tau \) = frac(2 I_0^2 R [1 - \( - 1 \)^n], n pi L S rho c_p) sin^2 \( omega tau \) $<eq:solvedfouriercoeff> which gives $ z \( x \, t \; tau \) = sum_(n = 1)^oo frac(2 I_0^2 R [1 - \( - 1 \)^n], n pi L S rho c_p) sin^2 \( omega tau \)\
times med e^(- (n^2 / gamma + frac(h C, S rho c_p)) (t - tau)) sin frac(n pi x, L) $<eq:solvedz> when substituted back into Equation~(@eq:fourier). Finally, substituting Equation~(@eq:solvedz) into Equation~(@eq:impanalog) give the expression for the temperature variation $ Delta T \( x \, t \) = frac(2 I_0^2 R L, pi^3 kappa S) sum_(n = 1)^oo frac([1 - \( - 1 \)^n], 2 n^3) sin (frac(n pi x, L))\
times (frac(1, 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2)) - frac(1 / 2 e^(- 2 i omega t), 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2) - frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2)) - frac(1 / 2 e^(2 i omega t), 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2) + frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2))) $<eq:variation> where $h' = frac(h C, rho c_p S)$. $Delta T_(upright(a c)) \( x \, t \)$ corresponds to the terms in Equation~(@eq:variation) with dependence on $omega t$, which could be written as: $  & Delta T_(upright(a c)) \( x \, t \) = frac(2 I_0^2 R L, pi^3 kappa S) sum_(n = 1)^oo frac(- 1 + \( - 1 \)^n, 2 n^3) sin frac(n pi x, L)\
 & times #scale(x: 240%, y: 240%)[\[] frac(1 / 2 exp \( - 2 i omega t \), 1 + 1 / n^2 h / kappa frac(C L^2, pi^2 S) - frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2)) + frac(1 / 2 exp \( 2 i omega t \), 1 + 1 / n^2 h / kappa frac(C L^2, pi^2 S) + frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2)) #scale(x: 240%, y: 240%)[\]] . $#label("eq:temp rise with gasAC") The remaining terms give $T_(upright(d c)) \( x \)$: $ Delta T_(upright(d c)) \( x \) = frac(2 I_0^2 R L, pi^3 kappa S) sum_(n = 1)^oo frac(1 - \( - 1 \)^n, 2 n^3) sin frac(n pi x, L) frac(1, 1 + 1 / n^2 h / kappa frac(C L^2, pi^2 S)) . $#label("eq:temp rise with gas")

To solve for $V_(3 omega)$, we first calculate the fluctuation of the resistance, $delta R$, that results from the fluctuation in temperature, $Delta T \( x \, t \)$, using the relation $ delta R = R' / L integral_0^L Delta T \( x \, t \) thin upright(d) x . $<eq:resistfluc> Substituting Equation~(@eq:variation) into Equation~(@eq:resistfluc) gives $ delta R = frac(2 I_0^2 R R' L, pi^3 kappa S) sum_(n = 1)^oo frac([1 - \( - 1 \)^n]^2, 2 n^4 pi)\
times med [frac(1, 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2)) - frac(1 / 2 e^(- 2 i omega t), 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2) - frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2)) - frac(1 / 2 e^(2 i omega t), 1 + frac(h' gamma, n^2) + frac(2 i omega gamma, n^2))] . $<eq:resistvariation> Keeping only the $n = 1$ term and multiplying the total resistance, $R + delta R$, by $I_0 sin \( omega t \)$ we obtain the 3$omega$ voltage across the suspended wire $ V_(3 omega) approx - frac(2 I_0^3 L R R', pi^4 kappa S) frac(sin \( 3 omega t - phi.alt' \), sqrt((1 + h / kappa frac(C L^2, pi^2 S))^2 + \( 2 omega gamma \)^2)) $#label("eq:V_3w with gas again") as presented in the main text. The above equations recover their vacuum case expressions in the limit $h = 0$ as presented by \[#link(<ref-Lu2001>)[13]\].

The above derivation requires that for any integer $n gt.eq 1$: $ n^2 / gamma + frac(h C, rho c_p S) gt.double frac(I_0^2 R', rho c_p L S) . $ For this to hold even in the case $h = 0$ and $n = 1$, we have the following constraint on the wire dimensions: $ d^2 / L gt.double frac(4 I_0^2 R', pi^3 kappa) . $ If $h > 0$, the constraint on the wire dimensions instead is $ d^2 / L + frac(4 h, kappa pi^2) L d gt.double frac(4 I_0^2 R', pi^3 kappa) . $ The wire diameter should also not exceed the thermal wavelength given by $lambda = sqrt(alpha \/ 2 omega)$ for our 1D heat-transfer description to hold. In other words, $ d lt.double sqrt(frac(kappa, 2 omega rho c_p)) . $ Since $gamma prop L^2$ a longer wire will result in a larger time constant. This imposes a practical constraint on the frequency window in experiment where $omega lt.double 1$ Hz becomes inconvenient to measure. A practical constraint on the wire length could therefore be given by requiring $gamma lt.double \( 1 med upright(s) \)$ as $ L^2 lt.double \( 1 med upright(s) \) frac(pi^2 kappa, rho c_p) . $ Note, that the chosen time of 1~s is an arbitrary but instructive choice. If $h > 0$, the above constraint instead reads $gamma_(upright(a p p)) lt.double \( 1 med upright(s) \)$ where $gamma_(upright(a p p)) = frac(gamma, 1 + h / kappa frac(4 L^2, pi^2 d))$. It follows that $ frac(L^2, 1 + h / kappa frac(4 L^2, pi^2 d)) lt.double \( 1 med upright(s) \) frac(pi^2 kappa, rho c_p) . $

