Plugging $T_(upright(b))$ back into the $Q_(upright(w))$ expression yields $ Q_(upright(w)) = frac(4 kappa_(upright(g a s)) L alpha_(upright(w)) Phi, frac(l_(upright(m f p)) pi, \( d + 2 l_(upright(m f p)) \) arcsin (frac(d, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) + 2 / pi alpha_(upright(w)) Phi ln (frac(r_0, d \/ 2 + l_(upright(m f p))))) \( T_(upright(w)) - T_0 \) . $ Finally, using $h = Q_(upright(w)) \/ \[ pi d L \( T_(upright(w)) - T_0 \) \]$, we obtain $ h = frac(kappa_(upright(g a s)), frac(d l_(upright(m f p)) pi^2, 4 alpha_(upright(w)) Phi \( d + 2 l_(upright(m f p)) \) arcsin (frac(d, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) + d / 2 ln (frac(2 r_0, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) . $<eq:hmodel> For $l_(upright(m f p)) gt.tilde d \/ 2$, we can use $arcsin (frac(d, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p)))) approx frac(d, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p)))$ to simplify Eq.~(@eq:hmodel) as follows: $ h approx frac(kappa_(upright(g a s)), frac(l_(upright(m f p)) pi^2, 4 alpha_(upright(w)) Phi) + d / 2 ln (frac(2 r_0, d + 2 l_(upright(m f p))))) . $ In the continuum limit, $l_(upright(m f p)) lt.double d$ and $K n arrow.r 0$, Equation~(@eq:hmodel) becomes $ h = frac(2 kappa_(upright(g a s)), d ln (frac(2 r_0, d))) . $<eq:hmodelcont> This expression immediately follows from the textbook solution $Q_(upright(w))$ of Fourier's law applied to steady-state radial thermal conduction in a hollow cylinder with thermal conductivity $kappa_(upright(g a s))$, an inner wall of radius $d \/ 2$ at temperature $T_(upright(w))$, and an outer wall of radius $r_0$ at temperature $T_0$ \[#link(<ref-Bergman2017>)[44]\], when similarly requiring $h = Q_(upright(w)) \/ \[ pi d L \( T_(upright(w)) - T_0 \) \]$. The same continuum limit is reached by a model proposed for carbon nanotubes \[#link(<ref-Wei2013b>)[45], #link(<ref-Wei2013>)[46]\].

While the above model relies on thermal conduction in the gas phase, $h$ extracted from our measurement is agnostic to the nature of the underlying heat transfer (whether it is conductive or convective). To test for indications of convective enhancement, we have calculated the Nusselt number $N u = h \/ \( kappa_(upright(g a s)) \/ d \)$ based on $h$ determined from our experiment. Here we assume $kappa_(upright(g a s)) = 0.026$~W/(m$dot.op$K). The values of $N u$ thus determined are shown in @fig:A2. These values compare very well with data reported by \[#link(<ref-peinado2021free>)[47]\] for free convection heat transfer for Pt wires of similar diameters (12.7 $mu$m and 25 $mu$m) measured using a hot wire method in rarified gas atmosphere. In particular, they report $N u approx 0.37$ at ambient pressure ($K n approx 3 times 10^(- 3)$), which is in good agreement with our value of $N u = 0.44 plus.minus 0.02$. Our $med 20 %$ greater result could be an indication that the actual heat transfer to the gas is slightly enhanced over pure conduction in our experiment.


